# Example: putting Radd behind an EXISTING Caddy that already owns :443.
#
# Common when one public IP is already forwarded to a working reverse proxy and
# you don't want to disturb it. The edge terminates TLS and forwards plain HTTP
# to Traefik; all routing beyond that lives in Ingress resources, so this file
# should never need editing again.
#
# Pin Traefik's `web` entrypoint to a known NodePort first — see
# docs/deploy-k3s.md. Replace the address with your k3s node's.

radd.example.com {
	# Caddy appends the peer to X-Forwarded-For automatically, which is what
	# lets Radd's clientip.py recover the real client address after the second
	# hop — provided RADD_TRUSTED_PROXIES lists BOTH this Caddy and the pod
	# CIDR. It also sets X-Forwarded-Proto from the incoming scheme, so the
	# cluster sees "https" despite the inner hop being plain HTTP.
	#
	# Large uploads need no tuning: Caddy v2 ships no default write/idle
	# timeout and no request body cap, and reverse_proxy streams. (Timeouts,
	# if you ever want them, are a global `servers { timeouts { … } }` option,
	# not a site directive.)
	reverse_proxy 10.0.0.20:30080
}
